WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.510
- This week on Couch Tomatoes,

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:04.340
we're talking The
Mandalorian from Disney Plus

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.820
and giving you our Fresh Picks
on what else to watch on TV.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.040
Hello, hello friends.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.330
Welcome back to Couch Tomatoes

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.690
sponsored by Freschetta pizza.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:12.900
I'm your host Naz Perez.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.120
And today, on the show, we're diving deep

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.300
into the first live-action
"Star Wars" series.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.670
It's called the Baby Yoda Show.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:21.190
I'm just kidding, it's
called "The Mandalorian"

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.820
but we're definitely talking
about Baby Yoda later today.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.920
And here to help me talk
about it is the author

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.380
of "Why We Love "Star Wars"", Ken Napzok,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.700
Collider's Perri Nemiroff

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.080
and Fandango's film and TV
correspondent, Nikki Novak.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.027
Hello, hello.
- Hi!

00:00:33.027 --> 00:00:34.010
- Hello!
- Hi!

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:34.843
- Guys, I gotta tell you

00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:37.420
I'm so happy you're here just
'cause I'm a prequels lover

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.370
and kid at heart so I feel
like much to learn I have,

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.940
I am but a young padawan,
amongst your presence, Ken.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:44.820
- No, I love it.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.740
I actually coined the term
Prequelist and we are strong.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.150
We are those you who look
past a lot of silly things

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.570
to find the themes that
George put in there

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.520
and those things are
present in The Mandalorian.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.310
- Wow, I feel like I've
just been knighted by you.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.290
Okay, guys let's jump right in to it.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.710
In a galaxy, not that
far away from our couch,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.480
The Mandalorian is set
five years after the events

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.270
of Return of the Jedi and the
fall of the Galactic Empire.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.440
And we know the show
follows a lone bounty hunter

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.050
known as The Mandalorian.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.780
Now, Ken if you're fan of the films,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.510
we know Jango and Boba as bounty hunters.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.720
We're familiar with them
but is there anything about

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.750
The Mandalorian's that we need to know

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.710
or should know going in
to this that's out there?

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.710
- Well, the first thing
that you should know

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.990
is that Jango and Boba
aren't actually Mandalorians.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.220
They are accused.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.690
Jango's been accused of stealing the armor

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.990
and running around pretending
to be a Mandalorian.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.990
Now, that could turn out to
be something totally different

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.140
but the culture is very war-like.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:42.120
They love their violence
but then Duchess Satine,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.570
during the Clone Wars
era, believed in peace

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.060
and started a movement of peace

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.430
and that kinda became the culture.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.860
There's a lot of pride
and honor in that outfit,

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.400
in those armor, the
armors of The Mandalorian.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.360
- That's really interesting
cause I feel like most people

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.420
are just categorizing "The
Mandalorian" with Boba and Jango.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.990
But what I love about this show is that

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:02.500
the creator Jon Favreau
and the EP, Dave Filoni

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.240
has said, that you don't
need to be a "Star Wars" fan

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.380
to jump into this and
hop on the bandwagon.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.620
But what are you guys loving
about the show so far?

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.550
- I couldn't agree more
with that statement.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.250
I mean, that's one of my
favorite things about the show

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.960
to start is how narrow the focus is.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.160
As much as I love the galaxy far far away

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.930
and all the possibilities there are in it,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.140
the fact that we have
this one main character

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.190
and we're getting to,

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.310
I mean, essentially live
with him for a little

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.490
and experience his day to day life.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.130
I feel like that's part of the reason

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.470
the show's kicked off so strong.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.320
- What I love about it is that

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:33.680
it really does have its own feel.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.910
Although, it's steep in
the "Star Wars" universe,

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.930
there's a hundred thousand
Easter eggs for people to find,

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.680
it really does feel like its own thing.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.770
I love that it just kind
of unfolding slowly.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.730
You know, I remember
from "The Force Awakens",

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.060
you jumped right into the action,

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.660
this, sort of like takes its time

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.910
and you're really getting
invested in the characters.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.190
- I love it and we were
actually talking about earlier

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.130
how I love the fact that it's episodic.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.160
So, it comes out week to week

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.960
and I just feel like
"Star Wars" is an event.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.990
We wanna talk about
this stuff week to week.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.870
And Nikki and I actually sat down earlier

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.890
and had the ultimate debate
on whether it's better

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.440
to binge an entire series
or watch it week to week.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:06.350
Take a look.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.030
So, Nikki, today's show got me thinking

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.620
about how I like to watch shows.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.380
Do you like watching one episode a week

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.320
like the good old days

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.820
or do you like binging an
entire season in one seating?

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.690
- Oh, binging for sure.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.840
I'm way too impatient
to wait week to week.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.340
I love seeing everything in
one seating, how about you?

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.150
- I actually like watching
one episode a week

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.300
because I feel like it gives me something

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.133
to look forward to.

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:26.350
But I also love just
inviting friends over,

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.850
tossing in a pizza and then
talking about the episode

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:29.683
with them after.

00:03:29.683 --> 00:03:31.063
- It makes way more sense
than the way I do it.

00:03:31.063 --> 00:03:32.477
(laughing)

00:03:32.477 --> 00:03:34.620
It makes it more of a
group experience, more fun.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.170
- It does and it doesn't
have to be anything formal.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.680
Whenever they come over I
love serving Freschetta pizza

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.350
because they have so
many different flavors

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.570
so, I'm able to mix it up
and make the episode special.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.700
- So, what are you serving
this week and am I invited?

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.060
- You are always invited.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.130
And this week, I am serving
the gluten free pepperoni.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.190
The pepperoni is so good
and zesty and no joke,

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.590
the gluten free crust tastes
just like regular pizza.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.423
- I want it now.

00:03:56.423 --> 00:03:57.256
Can we just have it now?

00:03:57.256 --> 00:03:58.505
- Let's dig in.
- Okay.

00:03:58.505 --> 00:03:59.490
(laughing)

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.260
- All right guys, so at
the time of this recording,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.320
we've all only seen two episodes, so far.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.470
If you haven't seen Chapters
One and Two of The Mandalorian,

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.390
this is your official spoiler alert.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:12.960
Chapter One is 88% on the
Tomatometer, 39 minutes long.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.950
Chapter Two, 93% on the Tomatometer

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.150
just a short 32 minutes long.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.900
Which brings up an interesting question,

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:23.020
is "Star Wars" better in smaller
bites or on the big screen?

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.550
What do you guys think?

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.130
- Look, "Star Wars" is
an epic event, right?

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.140
Even Bob Iger talks about
keeping that intact with movies

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.810
But the audience's,

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.460
what we want out of
stories now has changed.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.877
I always look at shows, go
back to "Lost", "Sopranos",

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:41.720
"Game of Thrones" is the big
example of we wanna take time

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.930
and they're forcing us, as fans,

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.950
to take time with these characters.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.010
You're talking about binge or not.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.100
It's like, they're like, nope
you're gonna have patience.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.100
You're gonna enjoy this
story over the course

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.890
of short little bites and it's working

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.210
and I never thought I'd be
saying that as a "Star Wars" fan.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.150
- I thought it was really
interesting when the first episode

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.130
when I start press play
on the first minute,

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.330
it's so beautiful and
it has a cinematic feel.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.100
I felt like, oh my gosh, you poor thing

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.410
being seen on this little screen,

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.240
you deserve a big screen
in this cinematic.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.610
But that's what I love about it

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.820
is that it is cinematic quality
but we can take the time.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:17.740
I mean, we just want more "Star Wars".

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.960
So, I think, whether
or not this turns into

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.613
then a film adaptation, I think
it's all good, it's all up.

00:05:23.613 --> 00:05:25.570
- It's the best time to be a "Star Wars"

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:26.600
fan for that reason.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.300
We get the best of both worlds.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.040
We still have films on
the way, eventually,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.220
after "Rise of Skywalker"
but now, we're gonna have

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.030
all of this Disney Plus
content to fill those gaps.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:39.360
And the idea of having these mini stories

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.640
with the same character so far are proving

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.410
to be extremely effective.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.300
- [Naz] Right, it gets
you even more excited

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.610
and us even as "Star Wars"
fans are learning more sides

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.850
to this story we didn't
even for example, Baby Yoda.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.570
Let's talk about the little
green elephant in the room.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.720
This thing is so cute, I
don't know if I wanna eat it

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.963
or punch it in the face.
- We know what it wants to eat

00:05:58.963 --> 00:05:59.820
- It is the cutest thing I've ever seen

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.700
in the entire world.
- It wants to eat frogs.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:02.900
Which I did not appreciate that,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.290
it's my biggest fear in life.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:05.540
- I was dissecting--
- You lost me there.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:06.530
- The whole time, I'm like,

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:08.656
oh is that how it gets its green color?

00:06:08.656 --> 00:06:11.547
What do we know about the Yoda species?

00:06:11.547 --> 00:06:12.960
- Wait, I wanna know Nikki
are you afraid of frogs

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.210
or eating frogs, which is the bigger fear?

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.700
- If you put one on the table right now,

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.151
I would be out of this room.

00:06:18.151 --> 00:06:20.090
- Oh well.
- It's my biggest fear.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.710
- We need a Baby Yoda around to eat it.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:23.985
This has been fascinating.

00:06:23.985 --> 00:06:28.720
The show went in a real
interesting direction

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.500
to be like, oh you thought
it was just gonna be about

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.100
a Mandalorian bounty hunter

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.220
in the underworld of "Star Wars".

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.130
No, this has ramifications
for the franchise.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:37.910
Ramifications for the fandom.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.580
I really think this is gonna lead

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:42.400
to us learning the biggest
secret in "Star Wars"

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.110
which is Yoda's species.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:47.980
And I didn't expect that in
a live action "Star Wars".

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.150
We're focused on other
things and then this pops up.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.910
And there's a place for
cute in "Star Wars".

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:53.200
- Yeah
- There always needs

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:54.520
to be cute.
- I'm excited to dive

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:55.410
into theories with you guys

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.790
but before, I just wanna know
what your initial thought was

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:59.870
when you saw it, was your mind blown?

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.430
'Cause people are going crazy.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.050
I mean, the internet is
calling it Baby Yoda.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.260
We obviously don't know if
it's even related to Yoda

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.410
but what were your thoughts
on it when you first saw it?

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:08.400
- You never know though.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.240
I think the timeline
could kind of work out

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.520
for it to be related to Yoda.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.760
But I remember sitting there
watching that first episode,

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.490
big smile on my face from start to finish.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.780
I was absolutely loving
it particularly the stuff

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.720
with the IG droid towards the end

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.290
but what took it from like it to love it

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.520
was the second I got that shot,

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.410
that reveal at the end,
that this character exists

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:34.480
and the way, that they've
kind of opt to that character

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.880
through episode two, that opens the door

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.980
to so many possibilities and I
think it's also possibilities

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.460
that could not only affect
the show going forward

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.400
but also maybe, "Rise of Skywalker" too.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.233
- Yup.

00:07:46.233 --> 00:07:48.455
- I think, it's the new Baby Groot.

00:07:48.455 --> 00:07:49.900
Maybe, he is the new Baby Groot.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:53.150
It's that universal, is there
anybody that doesn't love

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:55.640
what we're calling Baby Yoda, for now.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.260
But I think in episode two,
what I really appreciated was

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.990
seeing that relationship start
to build with the Mandalorian

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:05.180
and how he's this warrior stoic character

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.930
and Baby Yoda is giving these looks

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:09.350
and trying to help him and heal him

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:10.880
and he's just not having any part of it.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:11.863
It was really intriguing to me because

00:08:11.863 --> 00:08:14.010
what I love about "Star
Wars" is the relationships

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.120
and that relationship to me
is so intriguing right now.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:19.190
I can't wait to see Baby
Yoda break him down.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:21.270
- I know, break him down.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.280
Well, you brought up how
he almost try to heal him

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.460
which we haven't seen The
Force ever heal someone.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.730
We're only two episodes in you guys.

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:31.340
Let's talk about theories,
speculation and expectation.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.170
Let's start with this Baby Yoda thing

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:34.770
'cause Perri you brought up a good point,

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.590
this could affect "The Rise of Skywalker"

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.880
And the way that this show,
the way that the episodes

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.160
of "The Mandalorian" is
releasing syncs up perfectly.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:44.350
Episode Seven comes out

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.640
the day before "The Rise of Skywalker".

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.000
Episode Eight comes out a week after.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.240
Is this Baby Yoda gonna
bring balance to the force

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.340
and maybe defeat Emperor Palpatine?

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:56.440
- I don't know
- Like what's happening?

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.830
- I don't know if I'm willing
to go that far just yet.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.870
But the fact that the run
of the show does bleed

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:06.010
into that movie, that's kind
of what you gotta do right now,

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:09.130
to play the game and to battle
all the other franchises

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.030
out there is have that connectivity.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.580
They're doing it with the
Marvel shows on Disney Plus.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:14.990
It would make sense for them to do it

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.607
with their "Star Wars" shows as well.

00:09:16.607 --> 00:09:19.730
When "Rise of Skywalker" is supposedly

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:22.270
about finding balance in the force,

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:24.027
having a character with force abilities

00:09:24.027 --> 00:09:27.530
like this Baby Yoda has already showed us.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.710
Also being of the Yoda
legacy to some extent,

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:34.570
whether this character is
actually related to Yoda or not,

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.290
that does seem to be like that key

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.210
that could be missing especially because

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:42.290
when we left these main
characters in "Last Jedi",

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.340
their forces were greatly diminished.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.650
They could use the help

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:48.270
and it seems like Baby Yoda
can be the one to swoop in

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.590
and maybe not save the day
but lend a little hand.

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:53.430
- And I was surprised
to see him use the force

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.860
in episode two but
let's go down that road.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.230
What are your speculation, expectations,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.180
do we think this is a
clone 'cause Dr. Pershing

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:03.400
had a clone emblem on his
suit, what do we think?

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.170
- I am very intrigued by the clone aspect

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:07.390
and the clone angle thing.

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:09.300
But Dave Filoni loves putting stuff in

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.920
and he's been helping craft
this show with Favreau.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.840
I think Dave likes to just drop
things into the canon water

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.890
and be like, have fun with that.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:21.560
But the idea, is it related
to Yoda, is it Yaddle's?

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.568
Did Yaddle and Yoda have
some, break some Jedi codes?

00:10:25.568 --> 00:10:28.481
(laughing)

00:10:28.481 --> 00:10:33.440
All I know is that, the
theme of a Mandalorian,

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.760
who's an orphan himself,
connecting with this orphan, now

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:36.920
is gonna lead us to somewhere.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.382
I just don't know if JJ
Abrams and Chris Terrio

00:10:39.382 --> 00:10:42.866
are watching that to figure
it out or were aware of it.

00:10:42.866 --> 00:10:44.953
So, I'm open to the big theories.

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:47.317
- Do you have any theories, Nikki?

00:10:47.317 --> 00:10:49.250
- I kind of concur with the two of you.

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:52.310
I don't know, I think the
cloning thing is an obvious,

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:54.640
that we're all gonna talk about
and then it's gonna end up

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.400
being, yeah sorry, I know
that's what you thought but, no.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:59.687
We're not gonna make that easy for you.

00:10:59.687 --> 00:11:01.540
- The Kamino patch is
such a big deal though.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:02.477
- Yeah.
- Oh, yeah.

00:11:02.477 --> 00:11:05.320
- And also because it's not
the only place we've speculated

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.570
about possible cloning too.

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:09.640
It's like, the Luke hand, the Rey thing.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.170
That's been a big theory
that's been floating around

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:13.550
for quite a while.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.610
If that doctor appeared in
"The Mandalorian" when he did

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:18.330
and had that kind of conversation

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.100
and has that kind of investment

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:22.950
in bringing back Baby Yoda alive,

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.540
one would think that he would want to do

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:29.003
what his old facility used
to do and clone a force user.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:33.977
It seems like such an
obvious path to make.

00:11:33.977 --> 00:11:37.430
- I'm becoming more
intrigued with who else

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:41.140
is sending the other bounty
hunters after Baby Yoda.

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:42.013
- [Naz] Like IG-11.

00:11:42.013 --> 00:11:45.050
- IG-11, the Trandoshan
brothers, for all I know.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.130
- Yeah.
- They all have a tracker.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:48.100
Who else is sending it?

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:49.410
I don't think it's just The Client,

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:50.460
Werner Herzog's character.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:51.720
I think there's someone else there.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.380
That's where my theory mind is going.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:57.070
Who knows, maybe Boba
Fett is behind it all?

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.080
Just like, you know what,
I'm still in the game.

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:00.853
I need this big score, I don't know.

00:12:00.853 --> 00:12:02.570
That's pretty exciting for me.

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:04.350
- That's another theory
running in the internet too

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:06.440
is people say, you know, Boba died.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:09.250
He fell in the pit but we
never actually see him die.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:12.920
Is it possible that Boba
Fett is The Mandalorian?

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:14.950
- No, I don't think he is The Mandalorian

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:19.090
but in modern canon, in the
"Aftermath" novel series

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.600
by Chuck Wendig, you'll do learn the armor

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.540
is there in the Sarlacc pit.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.360
A character named Cobb Vanth
takes it and puts it on

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:30.940
but there was no body, so, who knows?

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.200
- Ken, I'm glad brought
up the "Aftermath" Trilogy

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:34.870
so for those of you guys watching,

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.377
they're a set of novels
that take place between

00:12:37.377 --> 00:12:40.100
"Return of the Jedi"
and "The Force Awakens".

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:42.100
Do you think that they're
taking any other source material

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:44.030
from those books and
putting it into the show?

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:46.230
- I think they're taking
the tones and the themes

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:47.230
and the world building.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:48.530
I think what's going on in this,

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.500
we're five years after Jedi,

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:52.270
the New Republic's trying
to figure out what to do,

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:55.320
Mon Mothma's trying to
de-militarize the New Republic

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.360
and that's kinda gonna help lead the rise

00:12:58.360 --> 00:12:59.830
of these people looking for identity.

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:01.440
This show's about identity.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.460
The empire needs identity post fall.

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:06.080
Cara Dune is a rebel soldier

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:07.770
who needs an identity without a war.

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.750
He is a bounty hunter
who needs his identity

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:10.660
back as a Mandalorian.

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:12.500
And I think, that's where
some of the bigger things

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.127
will come out of the show.

00:13:14.127 --> 00:13:16.230
- "Aftermath" is a great read for anybody

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:17.750
who hasn't read those books.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:19.410
But I don't know how much they're pulling

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:20.400
from the books, specifically

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:24.250
just because they never
really have to a degree,

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:27.070
as far as new characters
and new information goes.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:29.040
But I don't know, I'm semi-entertaining

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.710
the Boba Fett idea, even though I.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:33.410
If I had a bet right now, I would say,

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:36.320
no, he's not Boba Fett but
there are certain things

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.560
out there that do align quite well.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.100
The time period is very very appropriate.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:46.240
So, it could, the fact that
he falls into the Sarlacc pit,

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:49.790
the armor does still exist
but even if it was chewed up

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:52.130
he would need new armor and
it does kind of look like,

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.940
we already saw them replace
the shoulder with the Beskar

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.480
so he does clearly need new armor

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:59.110
so it could have been
because of that event.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.200
- And I heard we learn
more about the Beskar steel

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:03.450
in the third episode from
friends in the building

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:04.880
that have seen it before.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.374
Have you guys seen the third episode yet?

00:14:06.374 --> 00:14:07.890
- I have not.
- Not yet.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.130
- I need your friends.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:10.715
(laughing)

00:14:10.715 --> 00:14:12.560
- I feel like the third
episode's a turning point

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.050
that all of us on the
couch have missed out on.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:16.840
- Well, let's talk about
other things that we do know.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:18.870
We know that this Baby
Yoda is 50 years old.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:19.910
Ken, where would that put it?

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:22.350
Was this thing born before
"Phantom Menace", then?

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:23.640
- Could be around that time.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:24.890
The "Star Wars" math.

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:28.050
You're 25 years away from
"Revenge of the Sith",

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:30.380
which means you're there, you know,

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.870
you got about 15 years going
back to Phantom Menace.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.620
My "Star Wars" math gets a little fuzzy

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:37.303
but it's around that time.

00:14:37.303 --> 00:14:38.136
- [Nikki] I'm glad you did the math.

00:14:38.136 --> 00:14:40.120
- Yeah, I didn't do it well
but it's around that time.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.901
- What about other great
characters we've seen?

00:14:41.901 --> 00:14:44.030
Do we think IG-11 is gone for good or--

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:46.640
- We were talking about that
before we were theorizing.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:47.900
I mean, you have Taika Waititi.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:49.530
You have one episode.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:51.840
Instantly, the second he
came on screen, you're like,

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:55.460
need more of this but it's
just like them to say,

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:57.640
especially Taika and
knowing Taika's personality,

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:58.910
no, we're just gonna give you a bit.

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.815
I know you want more but
no it's not gonna happen.

00:15:00.815 --> 00:15:01.650
- That would be such a shame.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.560
That fight sequence was so good.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:08.410
And the IG droids are just,
they're so visually stimulating.

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:11.310
It's so much fun to watch them in action.

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:13.300
Maybe we'll get another IG droid.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:14.610
It doesn't have to be IG-11.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:16.660
There are other bounty
hunter droids out there,

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:18.460
who knows maybe we can
get a different one.

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.670
- Can I have a selfish
question to all of you guys?

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.400
Are his eyes like what he shoots out of?

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.110
This bounty droid, I'm
like, where are his eyes?

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.164
- I'm trying to, see we're all learning.

00:15:28.164 --> 00:15:32.480
Favreau is been writing
the series since the 80's.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:34.730
He dumped his can of figures on the floor

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:36.840
and said what if, what
if, and that's where,

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:39.660
we're finally getting to see
an IG series move like we have

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:42.300
'cause in "Empire Strikes Back",
it's a prop, sitting there

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.690
that's just, you can't move it, so.

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:46.780
The fluid, the movement,
he has some character

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:50.160
in those eyes, I wouldn't say no.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.350
I wouldn't trust that droid

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:52.930
to not shoot me from his eyes.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.090
(laughing)

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:56.860
- And Carl Weathers' character Greef Karga

00:15:56.860 --> 00:16:00.590
mentions a smuggler and the
way he says the word, smuggler

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:04.280
makes me think, are we
gonna get a Han Solo cameo

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:06.443
in "The Mandalorian",
what do you guys think?

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:10.150
- I would say no, at this point.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:13.970
I have a feeling that's not gonna happen.

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:14.910
Now, that I'm saying that,

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.510
I'm thinking about the swell of support

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:21.020
for Alden Ehrenreich to
return as Solo at some point.

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:22.900
But I mean, it would kind of make sense

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:24.670
because we're on an outer rim planet

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:27.100
and he could be out in that region.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:29.760
But I still think that it
goes back to what we're saying

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.090
at the very beginning of this episode,

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:32.900
that one of the main benefits
that they have right now

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:34.310
is how contained this world is

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:37.780
and how unique it makes it
feel while also maintaining

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:40.030
the pillars of what make
"Star Wars", "Star Wars".

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:42.360
I think it would be in their benefit

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:45.800
to play in that little sandbox
for a little while longer.

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:47.150
- Let's talk about Easter eggs, guys

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.070
'cause there is a lot of them in here.

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:50.510
So, what have been your
favorite Easter egg

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:51.710
so far, in the show?

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:55.230
- For me, it was the No
Disintegrations thing

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:58.370
which, you know, Vader says to Boba Fett

00:16:58.370 --> 00:16:59.203
in "Empire Strikes Back",

00:16:59.203 --> 00:17:00.840
and you just kinda grow up imagining

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:03.140
what that might be in your head.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:05.550
And even though again, not
necessarily is this is Boba Fett

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:08.500
but the weapon comes from Boba
Fett's holiday special weapon

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:10.980
so to see it and I feel
sorry for those Jawas

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:12.453
but it was so cool.

00:17:13.322 --> 00:17:14.730
Like I feel sorry for them but I was like,

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.060
do it again, do it again!

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:18.350
- The jawas had like
the worst brunch ever.

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:19.860
That egg, I was like, what?

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:21.930
I thought there was gonna be
something so cool in that egg.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:23.290
It was like mac and cheese

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:25.133
or something.
- Something magical

00:17:25.133 --> 00:17:28.100
- I think the Kamino patch is still my top

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:29.530
because I didn't know that.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:31.230
And then, I think it was a Reddit user

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.530
that first found it and
I'm like, you're right!

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:34.560
And that could change everything!

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:35.393
And I freaked out.

00:17:35.393 --> 00:17:38.530
But also, just seeing the
Jawas get so much screen time

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:40.400
because that was always just

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:42.560
like a very visually pleasing character.

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:43.730
I like cute creatures too

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:45.670
and they're cute and I
like the noises they make

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:49.410
but we only got the tiniest
little taste of them.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:51.520
And now, all of a sudden,
in this one episode,

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:54.870
we see the inner workings
of the Sandcrawler,

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:57.070
so many little features
that we never knew before.

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.770
And like yeah, laugh at the egg thing

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.250
but that was clearly something
that was very important

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:02.860
to this epic "Star Wars" race

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:04.780
that we never really knew before.

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:07.740
The fact that this series
is giving us a little time

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:10.770
to live with not just the
Mando but others as well,

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:11.910
yes, I want more of that.

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:14.180
- I know.
- I love the use of Carbonite.

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:17.050
I was saying that in Empire,
I watched that as a child

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:19.870
and it terrified me, the
use of Carbonite freezing

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:22.030
and the fact that they brought that back,

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:24.240
I thought in the first episode.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:25.930
And yeah, to me,

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.760
that was just all that
"Star Wars" nostalgia.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.850
It was like a little extra
and I can't wait to see

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:33.580
how they're gonna use
it more moving forward.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:34.870
- I know, I love the frozen Carbonite.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.160
I wish I could freeze my own body

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:37.390
and then, hang it on the wall.

00:18:37.390 --> 00:18:40.124
I always thought it
looked so cool. (laughs)

00:18:40.124 --> 00:18:42.470
But I have to ask you guys,
'cause I know you said

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.430
there's no better time than
to be a "Star Wars" fan

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:47.610
but is "Star Wars" fatigue a real thing?

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:51.160
- It's not for a lot of
people because I think

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.600
there still general audience
who just likes to take it in

00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:56.100
as part of an entertainment buffet.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:59.630
I think that there's some
truth to that a little bit

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:03.950
but I mean, I got "Star Wars"
pins on my jacket, I'm okay.

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:06.840
But I'm not the general
audience so, I don't know.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:08.790
- Maybe, I'm not the
general audience either

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.540
but I don't feel it in the least.

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:13.640
And I'm someone who is going
out and watching the movies,

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:16.240
watching the shows, watching
the animated series,

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:18.950
I read a lot of the books, I love it

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:21.370
and I love it because the world is so big.

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:23.670
Yes, the Skywalkers
have been our main focus

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:24.990
for a very very long time

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:28.590
but there are so many little
corners that really now

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:32.590
at least on the wider scale,
we're finally exploring.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:34.910
I definitely highly
recommend people to check out

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:37.880
the comics and the books to
find out those little nuggets

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:39.150
that they might not have seen before.

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:40.970
- I think that's what
this show is showing you

00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:43.540
is that there are so many
more nuggets to explore.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:45.530
So rather than being
fatigued, it makes me go,

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:47.490
oh wait, they could also
go in this direction

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:49.890
or this direction, I think
there's just even more now.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:53.090
- Well guys, we are just barely
scratching the surface here.

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:55.180
Obviously, we could go
on for hours and hours.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:57.270
So, hopefully we answered
some of your guys' questions

00:19:57.270 --> 00:19:59.990
if we didn't, maybe join
Bob Iger's masterclass,

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.090
maybe he'll answer them
for you guys. (laughs)

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:03.260
But I can't have you leave

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:04.900
without giving me your Fresh Picks.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.930
So, these are just shows
on TV that you are watching

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:09.880
that you think people at
home should tune in to.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:10.773
- All right, are you ready?
- Yeah!

00:20:10.773 --> 00:20:12.570
- This is weird.
- Okay?

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:14.610
- I love watching British gardening shows.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.973
- Yes!
- What? (laughs)

00:20:16.973 --> 00:20:18.880
- You need to watch, "Love Your Garden"

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:21.120
with Alan Titchmarsh
over there in England.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:24.370
He goes to people in need
and re-does their garden.

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:25.550
And if you're not crying

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:28.770
by the time the sunflowers are
growing, you're not a human.

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:31.140
And I watch that all the time.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:33.260
And then, "Monty Don" which is like this,

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:36.490
he's this professor, wears
like a cardigan and boots

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:38.550
and then he goes in the
yard and British gardens

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:40.880
and just, I'm obsessed with it.

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:42.850
- I was not expecting
to hear that from you.

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.010
Where can we find "Love Your Garden"?

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.400
- Netflix has a run on it and
the "Monty Don" stuff too.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:49.743
- I have a question, if you
had to choose between that

00:20:49.743 --> 00:20:52.940
and "Star Wars" what
would you choose? (laughs)

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:55.488
- You know what, we all
need to love our garden

00:20:55.488 --> 00:20:56.550
- Wow!

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.400
- I have a little Death Star planter

00:20:58.400 --> 00:20:59.833
that I won't be able to
grow for the life of me,

00:20:59.833 --> 00:21:01.237
maybe I should gift it to you.

00:21:01.237 --> 00:21:03.620
- Death Star planter (laughs)
- I swear.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:04.970
I bought it at "Star Wars" celebration.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:06.570
- That's the great gift for you.

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:11.090
- My pick is, a YouTube
original show, it's "Impulse".

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:12.860
And it's basically the show

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:14.840
that they said was a
spin-off from "Jumper".

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:16.680
And yes, it is to a degree

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.700
but it's a completely
different set of characters

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:21.330
and it handles that ability
in a very unique way.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.690
But it's got one of the best
lead performances I've seen

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:27.550
in a new show in years,
her name is Maddie Hasson.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:29.640
She is phenomenal in it.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:34.300
So, it is a show with a super
cool supernatural ability

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.050
but what makes that come
out of her in her real life

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:38.420
she's capable of doing that

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:40.660
is a very traumatizing experience.

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.640
It's a very dark show

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:45.800
and she handles that emotion so so well.

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:48.310
I think she is gonna be
one of the next best things

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:50.920
but until she gets more
and more opportunities,

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:52.970
I think "Impulse" is well worth a watch.

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:54.380
It's a great show across the board.

00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:55.213
- And on YouTube.

00:21:55.213 --> 00:21:56.950
- Yes on YouTube.
- Amazing, thanks Perri.

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:58.591
- Nikki, what are you watching?

00:21:58.591 --> 00:21:59.910
- I think mine is as much
a "Star Wars" departure

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:01.720
as British gardening is.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.960
I chose "Dickinson" which is on Apple TV

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.750
and Hailee Steinfeld, who
I think did a great job

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:10.600
in "Bumblebee", she's the lead in it.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:12.830
It's the story of Emily
Dickinson but it's told

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:16.130
in a very very modern way
and with modern themes.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:19.020
And it's one of those, there's 10 episodes

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.220
and it's just easy to binge.

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:23.473
Like I found myself on episode
10 before I knew it going,

00:22:23.473 --> 00:22:25.280
how did that happen?

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:26.520
It's very digestible.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:28.360
- Yeah and I love that
all 10 episodes are out

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:29.522
for that one now.
- Yes.

00:22:29.522 --> 00:22:31.157
- Well, since we're talking about

00:22:31.157 --> 00:22:33.000
"The Mandalorian" on Disney Plus,

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:36.280
I am obsess with "The World
According to Jeff Goldblum".

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:38.790
I can watch this man do anything.

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:40.960
The first episode is about sneakers.

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:43.370
He dives into the world of
sneakers and I love sneakers.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:44.740
But there's a part in the episode

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:46.670
where he just plays street basketball

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:47.740
with these basketball players

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:50.120
and I feel like I'm watching
my dad try and go at it

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:53.310
and it is just worth the
money of Disney Plus alone

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:54.900
to watch Jeff Goldblum
try and play basketball.

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:55.733
- I heard somebody say,

00:22:55.733 --> 00:22:57.520
you don't learn anything about sneakers

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:59.700
but it's fascinating
to watch Jeff Goldblum

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:01.350
be fascinated about sneakers.

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:02.183
- Exactly.

00:23:02.183 --> 00:23:04.680
The whole time, he's just
like "Wow, wow, wow!"

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:05.690
I'm like dying laughing.

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:07.580
- There's like a weird
money deal that goes done

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:09.160
at that sneaker convention he's at.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:09.993
- Sneaker-Con, yes!
- Yeah.

00:23:09.993 --> 00:23:12.280
And just witnessing it,
it's one of the weirdest

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.430
wonderful things in TV.
- Yeah, I've been there.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:15.570
Buying the kicks.

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:16.677
All right guys, well that is our show.

00:23:16.677 --> 00:23:18.830
Again, thank you guys so much.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:22.440
Thanks to Ken and Perri and
Nikki for joining me today.

